const defaultPosts = [
  { id:'p1', slug:'new-york-convention-primer', title:'The New York Convention: A Practitioner’s Primer', subtitle:'Why the 1958 Convention remains the backbone of cross-border arbitration enforcement.', category:'Arbitration', tags:['New York Convention','Arbitration','Enforcement'], author:'Dr. Amara Okonkwo', date:'2026-10-12', updated:'2026-10-16', readTime:'8 min read',
    image:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1589829545856-d10d557cf95f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', featured:true, relatedCourse:'c1', published:true,
    content:`<p>The Convention on the Recognition and Enforcement of Foreign Arbitral Awards — commonly called the New York Convention — is one of the most successful international legal instruments in history. For practitioners, understanding its operation is essential.</p><h2>What the Convention does</h2><p>It obliges contracting states to recognise and enforce foreign arbitral awards and to refer parties to arbitration where a valid arbitration agreement exists. The framework is deliberately simple: enforce awards, respect agreements.</p><h2>The grounds for refusal</h2><p>The Convention lists a limited set of grounds on which enforcement may be refused. These include incapacity, invalid agreement, lack of notice, exceedance of scope, irregular composition, non-final awards, and public policy.</p><blockquote>The limited grounds for refusal are the Convention’s strength — they preserve finality.</blockquote><h2>Practical implications</h2><ul><li>Draft arbitration clauses that clearly identify the seat, rules, and tribunal.</li><li>Consider whether the relevant jurisdictions are contracting states.</li><li>Plan enforcement strategy early — do not leave it to the end of the dispute.</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions</h2><p>The Convention does not create a global arbitration code. It provides a framework for recognition and enforcement, which is supplemented by national law and institutional rules.</p><p>This article provides general information only. It does not constitute legal advice on any specific matter.</p>` },
  { id:'p2', slug:'gdpr-cross-border-transfers', title:'GDPR and Cross-Border Data Transfers: A Practical Map', subtitle:'The mechanisms that matter — and the mistakes that cost organisations most.', category:'Data & Privacy', tags:['GDPR','Data Transfers','Compliance'], author:'Dr. Sofia Lindqvist', date:'2026-10-04', updated:'2026-10-08', readTime:'9 min read',
    image:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516321318423-f06f85e504b3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', featured:false, relatedCourse:'c3', published:true,
    content:`<p>Cross-border data transfers are among the most operationally complex areas of privacy compliance. The rules are not just legal — they are architectural.</p><h2>Identify the transfer</h2><p>A transfer occurs when personal data is sent to, or accessed from, a jurisdiction outside the originating framework. Remote access can count.</p><h2>Choose the mechanism</h2><p>Adequacy decisions, Standard Contractual Clauses, and Binding Corporate Rules are the main transfer mechanisms. Each carries documentation and due-diligence obligations.</p><blockquote>The transfer mechanism is only as strong as the transfer impact assessment behind it.</blockquote><h2>Assess the destination</h2><p>Assess the legal environment of the destination jurisdiction, including government access and redress mechanisms.</p><h2>Document the analysis</h2><p>A transfer impact assessment should record the mechanism, the destination, the risks identified, and any supplementary measures.</p><p>This article provides general information. Always verify the current regulatory position and consult qualified counsel.</p>` },
  { id:'p3', slug:'drafting-arbitration-clauses', title:'Drafting Arbitration Clauses That Hold', subtitle:'Small clauses, large consequences — a checklist for transactional counsel.', category:'Cross-Border Contracts', tags:['Arbitration','Drafting','Contracts'], author:'Adv. Priya Menon', date:'2026-09-26', updated:'2026-09-28', readTime:'7 min read',
    image:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1450101499163-c8848c66ca85?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', featured:false, relatedCourse:'c2', published:true,
    content:`<p>An arbitration clause is often a single paragraph buried in a longer agreement. Yet it can determine where, how, and at what cost a dispute is resolved.</p><h2>Name the seat</h2><p>The seat determines the procedural law and the courts that supervise the arbitration. Ambiguity creates risk.</p><h2>Choose the rules</h2><p>Institutional rules provide a tested procedural framework. Ad hoc arbitration can work but requires careful drafting.</p><blockquote>A well-drafted clause removes arguments before they begin.</blockquote><h2>Address the tribunal</h2><p>Specify the number of arbitrators and, where appropriate, the method of appointment and qualifications.</p><h2>Language and governing law</h2><p>State the language of the arbitration and the law governing the agreement — these are not the same as the seat.</p><p>This article is general guidance. Transaction-specific review is essential.</p>` },
  { id:'p4', slug:'consumer-rights-ecommerce', title:'Consumer Rights in Cross-Border E-Commerce', subtitle:'What buyers and sellers should understand about jurisdiction and remedies.', category:'Consumer Protection', tags:['Consumer Rights','E-Commerce','Cross-Border'], author:'Adv. Rohan Desai', date:'2026-09-15', updated:'2026-09-18', readTime:'6 min read',
    image:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1453945619913-79ec89a82c51?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', featured:false, relatedCourse:'c4', published:true,
    content:`<p>Cross-border e-commerce raises a familiar question: whose consumer law applies? The answer is rarely simple.</p><h2>Jurisdiction</h2><p>Consumer contracts often include jurisdiction protections that override standard commercial clauses. The applicable regime may depend on the consumer’s residence.</p><h2>Remedies</h2><p>Remedies vary widely — refunds, replacement, compensation, and injunctive relief. What is available in one jurisdiction may be unavailable in another.</p><blockquote>Consumers should keep every receipt, communication, and delivery record.</blockquote><h2>Practical steps</h2><ul><li>Read the terms of service, particularly jurisdiction and governing law.</li><li>Document the transaction and any defect.</li><li>Identify the correct forum before filing.</li></ul><p>This article is general information only, not individualized legal advice.</p>` },
  { id:'p5', slug:'sovereign-immunity-disputes', title:'Sovereign Immunity in International Disputes', subtitle:'When states are parties, enforcement becomes a different game.', category:'Arbitration', tags:['Sovereign Immunity','Enforcement','State Contracts'], author:'Dr. Amara Okonkwo', date:'2026-09-02', updated:'2026-09-05', readTime:'10 min read',
    image:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1521791136064-7986c2920216?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', featured:false, relatedCourse:'c1', published:true,
    content:`<p>Disputes involving states raise distinct questions of immunity, waiver, and enforcement. The rules are not uniform across jurisdictions.</p><h2>Restrictive vs absolute immunity</h2><p>Many jurisdictions adopt a restrictive approach: immunity applies to sovereign acts but not to commercial activity.</p><h2>Waiver clauses</h2><p>Express waiver clauses are often necessary but are not always sufficient. The scope and validity of waiver depend on the applicable law.</p><blockquote>Enforcement against state assets is where many awards meet their most difficult test.</blockquote><h2>Asset identification</h2><p>Identifying attachable assets requires careful analysis of what is commercial in nature and what is protected.</p><p>This article provides general information. State-specific analysis is essential.</p>` },
  { id:'p6', slug:'plain-language-international-contracts', title:'Plain Language in International Contracts', subtitle:'Clarity is not a lower standard — it is a risk-control measure.', category:'Legal Writing', tags:['Drafting','Clarity','Contracts'], author:'Adv. Sameer Qureshi', date:'2026-08-21', updated:'2026-08-24', readTime:'6 min read',
    image:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1436450412740-6b988f486c6b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80', featured:false, relatedCourse:'c6', published:true,
    content:`<p>Plain language does not mean imprecise language. It means choosing the clearest words that preserve the legal meaning.</p><h2>Write for a busy reader</h2><p>Lead with the operative rule. Use short paragraphs. Keep one idea per paragraph.</p><h2>Precision over tradition</h2><p>Retain technical terms where they carry a defined meaning. Remove archaic phrases that add length but not accuracy.</p><blockquote>Clarity is a professional courtesy and a risk-control measure.</blockquote><h2>Edit in passes</h2><ul><li>First pass: structure and argument</li><li>Second pass: sentence clarity</li><li>Third pass: citations and defined terms</li></ul><p>Good legal writing helps the reader understand the issue, the rule, the application, and the conclusion without unnecessary effort.</p>` }
